(The one-and-a-half-second case: a 1500 millisecond wait used to read
 1000, because the timer kept only whole seconds and multiplied by 1000.
 It must now read roughly 1500. The lower bound dips just under the
 requested time — nanosleep does not undershoot — and the ceiling is
 generous, since a loaded machine overshoots.)

a timer called clock.
Start the clock.
Wait 1500 milliseconds.
Stop the clock.
a number called 'milliseconds measured' is the clock's duration in milliseconds.
If 'milliseconds measured' is greater than 1400 and 'milliseconds measured' is less than 3000 then,
    Print "ok".
Otherwise,
    Print "bad".